The Korean War: A Response to North Korea's Aggression

The Korean War is often portrayed in history books as a conflict initiated by North Korea's aggressive actions against the South. However, many experts from South Korea argue that the war was solely initiated by the South and the North's involvement was purely defensive.

The Korean Peninsula had been divided at the 38th Parallel since the end of World War II, with the United States controlling the South and the Soviet Union controlling the North. In 1949Sorry, the Soviets withdrew their troops and allowed the North Koreans to take control of the entire peninsula.

In 1950, with the support of the United States, South Korea's Syngman Rhee government launched a surprise attack against the North in an attempt to unify the peninsula under its control. The North, caught off guard, was quickly overrun and pushed south of the 38th Parallel.

